---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base
  title="About - Nattskift Games"
  description="Nattskift means night shift. One person in Los Angeles designs, builds, and ships every game here - and keeps a short public list of things the studio will never do."
  ogImage="/og/about.png"
>
  <section class="section-pad">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-6">
      <h1 class="type-page-title">About the studio</h1>
      <div class="measure mt-10 space-y-5">
        <p class="type-lead">
          Nattskift means night shift. The studio is one person in Los
          Angeles, working the hours after everything else is done - which
          turns out to be a decent way to describe the whole approach.
        </p>
        <p class="text-muted">
          The day job is ordinary software. The games happen after it: Swift,
          SpriteKit, an iPhone on a cable, and whatever is left of the
          evening. That schedule sets the constraints, and the constraints
          set the design. Small scope. Systems over content. Nothing in a
          game that one person can't maintain at eleven at night.
        </p>
        <p class="text-muted">
          Three titles so far. A social slots casino with virtual coins, a survival game about keeping a fire lit, and a
          satire about the kind of studio this one refuses to become. They
          look unrelated. They are not.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="border-t border-hairline section-pad">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-6">
      <h2 class="type-section">Values, stated plainly</h2>
      <ul class="mt-10 max-w-3xl space-y-6">
        <li>
          <p class="font-medium text-text">Finished when it ships.</p>
          <p class="measure mt-1 text-muted">
            A release is a release, not an open beta with a marketing budget.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p class="font-medium text-text">The player is the customer.</p>
          <p class="measure mt-1 text-muted">
            Not the advertiser, not the acquisition funnel, not a spreadsheet
            of whales.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p class="font-medium text-text">Small on purpose.</p>
          <p class="measure mt-1 text-muted">
            One person means every decision has a name attached. All of them
            are the same name, which keeps the excuses short.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="how-the-money-works" class="border-t border-hairline section-pad">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-6">
      <h2 class="type-section">How the money works</h2>
      <div class="measure mt-8 space-y-4 text-muted">
        <p>
          Every game is free to start, so you can find out whether you like
          it without paying for the privilege. If you want more, there is one
          purchase, priced in real money, described in plain words before you
          tap. That's the model. There is no second, quieter model behind it.
        </p>
        <p>
          The trade-off, named out loud: this earns less than ads and timers
          would. The night shift doesn't run on that money anyway, so it can
          afford to be picky about where the money comes from.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="hire" class="border-t border-hairline section-pad">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-6">
      <h2 class="type-section">Hire the night shift</h2>
      <div class="measure mt-8 space-y-4 text-muted">
        <p>
          The same hands that build these games take outside work. Two kinds
          of project fit the schedule:
        </p>
      </div>
      <ul class="mt-8 max-w-3xl space-y-6">
        <li>
          <p class="font-medium text-text">Game development.</p>
          <p class="measure mt-1 text-muted">
            An iOS game built end to end — design, Swift and SpriteKit code,
            App Store submission. Your idea stays your property, and it ships
            finished, the same standard the studio holds its own games to.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p class="font-medium text-text">MVPs.</p>
          <p class="measure mt-1 text-muted">
            A first working version of your app or product — scoped honestly,
            built fast, and put in front of real users before you spend real
            money. You get something people can touch, not a deck about it.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
      <div class="measure mt-8 space-y-4 text-muted">
        <p>
          The trade-off, stated up front: one person means one project at a
          time — a short queue, no account managers, and the person you email
          is the person who writes the code.
        </p>
        <p>
          <a class="link-quiet" href="mailto:info@nattskiftgames.com?subject=Project%20inquiry">
            info@nattskiftgames.com</a> — say what you're making and where it
          honestly stands.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="border-t border-hairline section-pad">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-6">
      <h2 class="type-section">Things this studio will never do</h2>
      <ul class="mt-10 max-w-3xl space-y-3">
        {
          [
            'Sell ads, including "rewarded" ones.',
            'Add energy systems, lives, or timers that pause the game until you pay.',
            'Price anything in a made-up currency.',
            'Run limited-time offers on a loop and call them limited.',
            'Sell or share player data. There is none worth selling, by design.',
            'Ship a game to hit a date it isn\'t ready for.',
            'Say "we" in a press release.',
          ].map((line) => (
            <li class="border-l-2 border-hairline pl-4 text-muted">{line}</li>
          ))
        }
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
